\newlabel{fig:tpn}{{1}{4}{\textbf {TPN internal architecture and BudgetPredictor wiring.} Cross-attention with $H{=}4$ heads ($Q{=}\qemb $, $K{=}V{=}\vcoarse $) produces a context vector $\mathrm {ctx}{\in }\mathbb {R}^{384}$ and head-averaged spatial weights $\boldsymbol {\alpha }{\in }\mathbb {R}^{49}$. The $[\mathrm {ctx};\boldsymbol {\alpha }]$ concatenation passes through a Score MLP producing 49 coarse scores, which are bilinearly upsampled $7{\times }7\to 24{\times }24$ and instance-normalized to yield $\sN {\in }\mathbb {R}^{576}$. Phase 1 Adaptive shares $\mathrm {ctx}$ with the BudgetPredictor (orange) which emits the per-sample token fraction $k_{\mathrm {frac}}{\in }[0.0625, 0.5]$ consumed by the Adaptive Gumbel-STE}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{eq:gather}{{6}{4}{}{equation.6}{}}
\newlabel{eq:taufix}{{7}{4}{}{equation.7}{}}
\newlabel{sec:merge}{{3.6}{4}{}{subsection.3.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:ste}{{2}{5}{\textbf {Fixed-$k$ vs.\ Adaptive Gumbel-Sigmoid STE} (Phase 1 Final vs.\^^MPhase 1 Adaptive). \emph {Left:} the standard fixed-budget pipeline: scores + Gumbel noise feed both a soft sigmoid (for backward pass) and a hard top-$k{=}144$ selection (for forward pass), composed via the STE identity $\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {STE}}{=}\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {hard}}{-}\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {soft}}.\mathrm {detach}(){+}\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {soft}}$. \emph {Right:} the adaptive variant inserts a per-sample threshold $\theta $ derived from the BudgetPredictor's $k_{\mathrm {frac}}$ output. The shifted soft mask $\sigma ((\mathbf {n}{-}\theta )/\tau )$ produces a differentiable effective count $k_{\mathrm {eff}}{=}\sum _i m_{\mathrm {soft},i}$ that drives the budget loss; per-sample top-$k_{\mathrm {hard}}$ provides the hard mask with right-padding to $k_{\max }$ for batched processing}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{eq:assign}{{8}{5}{}{equation.8}{}}
\newlabel{eq:scoreweight}{{9}{5}{}{equation.9}{}}
\newlabel{eq:merge}{{10}{5}{}{equation.10}{}}
\newlabel{sec:adaptive}{{3.7}{5}{}{subsection.3.7}{}}
\newlabel{eq:bp}{{11}{5}{}{equation.11}{}}
\newlabel{eq:thresh}{{12}{5}{}{equation.12}{}}
\newlabel{fig:arch}{{3}{6}{\textbf {TC-ViTok system architecture with adaptive budget.} Three frozen encoders (purple) extract complementary representations: CLIP ViT-L/14 produces high-resolution patch tokens $\vfine $ for the LLM, DINOv2-S provides a coarse spatial map $\vcoarse $ for the TPN, and CLIP Text encodes the question into $\qemb $. The TPN (887K) cross-attends $\qemb $ over $\vcoarse $ and emits per-position scores $\sN $ plus a context vector $\mathrm {ctx}$. In adaptive mode, the BudgetPredictor (25K, orange) maps $\mathrm {ctx}$ to a per-sample token fraction. The Adaptive Gumbel-STE produces variable-length selection indices, which gate-and-merge $\vfine $ before the multimodal projector and LLaVA-1.5-7B+LoRA}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{tab:loss}{{2}{6}{Loss function components}{table.2}{}}
\newlabel{sec:loss}{{3.8}{6}{}{subsection.3.8}{}}
\newlabel{eq:loss}{{13}{6}{}{equation.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:merge}{{4}{6}{\textbf {Informative Token Merge (parameter-free).} \textbf {(a)} Each unselected token (gray) is soft-assigned to the most similar selected token (blue) via cosine similarity. Edge thickness encodes $\tilde {A}_{ij}{=}w_i\,\cos (v_i,v_j)$, where $w_i$ is the unselected token's TPN score (numbers below each token). \textbf {(b)} Merged outputs (gradient: selected~$\to $~unselected) preserve sequence length~$k$ but absorb background context from all $N{-}k$ discarded tokens. No new parameters}{figure.4}{}}
