\newlabel{sec:ethics}{{C}{19}{}{section.C}{}}
\newlabel{fig:qualitative-real}{{10}{19}{\textbf {TPN selection on six GQA val\_balanced examples.} Each row: raw image (left), top-$k{=}144$ selection overlay (right). The overlay tints only selected patches with warm colors; alpha is modulated by the per-image min-max normalized TPN score. Per-image normalization saturates the colormap on whichever positions rank highest \emph {within that image}, which can produce the visual impression of a regional bias. The aggregate-over-500-samples diagnostic (Fig.~\ref {fig:positional-prior}) shows the underlying pattern is heterogeneous and only mildly regional — much weaker than per-sample visualization suggests. We include these examples to make per-sample selection legible, not as evidence of question-conditioned focus, which the aggregate analysis refutes}{figure.10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:positional-prior}{{11}{20}{\textbf {Aggregate TPN selection diagnostics on 500 GQA val\_balanced samples.} \textbf {How to read:} compare the left panel to the uniform baseline $P_\text {select}{=}0.25$, then check whether the right panel has localized score depressions that explain the frequency map. \emph {Left:} per-position selection frequency $P_\text {select}$. Under fully content-driven selection at $k{=}144$, the uniform baseline is $144/576{=}0.25$ (per-position). The measured distribution is strongly heterogeneous (range 0--0.7, with 14\% of positions in a near-zero tail $P_\text {select}\!<\!0.02$): specific positions are selected on $>60\%$ of samples while others are selected on $<5\%$. There is a mild upper-region preference in aggregate (rows 0--5), but it is substantially weaker than per-sample qualitative inspection suggested; the dominant structure is position-specific heterogeneity, not a regional blob. \emph {Right:} per-image min-max normalized score, averaged across samples, is approximately uniform ($\bar {s}\!\approx \!0.65$) \emph {except} for a localized $\sim \!4{\times }4$-patch ``dead zone'' in the upper-center ($\bar {s}\!\approx \!0.35$, rows 0--4, cols 6--11) that the TPN systematically deprioritizes regardless of content. We conjecture this corresponds to an attention-sink position learned during joint training; testing this with register tokens~\cite {darcet2024vision} or sink masking is a direct follow-on. Together with the E3 ablation (\S \ref {sec:limits}, L7), these aggregate statistics give a geometric account of the convergent TPN behavior that per-sample qualitative inspection (Fig.~\ref {fig:qualitative-real}) cannot provide}{figure.11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:budget-collapse}{{12}{20}{\textbf {BudgetPredictor collapse vs.\ stable regime (fine-tune from warm-started checkpoint).} At $\lambda _b{=}0.03$, the head saturates against $k_{\max }{=}288$ within 10K steps and stays pinned; val-VQA simultaneously regresses from 0.3726 to 0.3898 (text in Section~\ref {sec:budget-collapse}). At $\lambda _b{=}0.10$, the head holds $k_\text {eff}\!\approx \!271$ for the full 20K-step horizon. The collapse boundary lies between these two values; the recommended operating window is $\lambda _b\!\in \![0.08,0.15]$}{figure.12}{}}
\newlabel{fig:oscillation}{{13}{20}{\textbf {Co-adaptation oscillation across pooler learning rates.} Validation VQA accuracy on the GQA val\_balanced subset for three Phase 1.5 trainable-pooler variants. \textbf {Top}: $\eta _\text {pooler}{=}5{\times }10^{-4}$ (v6), period $\approx $5K steps. \textbf {Middle}: $\eta _\text {pooler}{=}2{\times }10^{-5}$ (v8), period $\approx $15K steps; the trajectory is well-behaved through step 30K and deteriorates from step 35K onward. \textbf {Bottom}: $\eta _\text {pooler}{=}0$ (v8b, frozen pooler), no oscillation but the pooler outputs become fixed noise the LLM cannot use. The period scales as $1/\eta _\text {pooler}$, the empirical signature of the GAN-style co-adaptation loop diagnosed in Section~\ref {sec:co-adaptation}}{figure.13}{}}
